{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>The daughters are my daughters and the sons are my sons.</b> Our Rabbenu Ḥananel, of blessed memory, wrote that this is a lie of Laban in his saying 'and the sons are my sons,' for when the sons of sons are called 'sons,' they are indeed like sons, but they do not say thus concerning the sons of daughters. And likewise in what he said, 'and the flock is my flock,' when in fact he gave them to him as wages for his toil and labor; and likewise in what he said, 'and all that you see is mine' — and behold, Jacob said to him, 'With what of all the vessels did you find anything, of all the vessels of your house?' — and he found no answer. This matter is like the matter of Ben-hadad, who said to Ahab (1 Kings 20:3): ״Thus says Ben-hadad: your silver and your gold are mine, and your wives and your good sons are mine.״ And it is written: ״Know now and see that this one seeks evil.״" "<b>And behold.</b> Concerning Jacob's statement to Laban, 'Why did you pursue?' — after Laban answered, 'The daughters are my daughters,' for 'I came after you to see my daughters.'" "<b>And what shall I do for these my daughters today.</b> What good shall I do for them or for their sons whom they bore? But I shall do them this kindness: that you cut a covenant with me not to afflict them and not to take other wives upon my daughters." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״הבנות״ — two; ״בנותי״ — two; altogether four. From this it appears that even Bilhah and Zilpah were actually his daughters, from another wife or raised in his house."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:52.769700597Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}